  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Peter Lang Verlag in 2020, Margaret Buck's <em>Renewing the Church-State Partnership for Catholic Education</em> has accumulated four citations as of 2026, with uptake concentrated among UK-based scholars and institutions working on religious education and policy. Citations appear steadily across the citation window (2020, 2022, 2025, 2026), suggesting sustained engagement with the work's core themes rather than a spike of early attention.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Buck's monograph addresses a foundational question in education policy and religious studies: how Catholic institutions and state systems can renew partnership in a changing policy landscape. The work draws on concepts spanning political science, public administration, theology, and law\u2014a deliberately interdisciplinary scope reflected in its conceptual profile, which emphasizes general partnership and state governance alongside religious and philosophical dimensions.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly reception has been modest but geographically and topically coherent. All four citing works originate from United Kingdom researchers or institutions, primarily affiliated with St Mary's University Twickenham London and the University of Glasgow. This concentration suggests the work resonates with a specific scholarly community actively engaged in UK Catholic education policy and religious education reform. David Fincham appears as a recurring voice in this landscape, citing the work twice\u2014once in a 2025 reflective chapter on academic research in Catholic education, and again in a 2026 journal article in <em>Religions</em> that explicitly revisits the church-state relationship in light of the 1944 Butler Education Act.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing works themselves take different entry points into Buck's framework. Sean Whittle's 2020 article in the <em>Journal of Religious Education</em> evaluates the Catholic institutional response to the UK Commission on Religious Education\u2014an immediately policy-relevant application. David Lundie's 2022 book chapter examines school leadership in market contexts, bringing the partnership framework into conversation with managerial and governance concerns. Together, these citations indicate that the work is being mobilized to ground discussions of religious education governance, state policy, and institutional strategy rather than treated as a historical or archival reference.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Library holdings record four copies in open institutional catalogs, indicating modest but real presence in teaching and research collections. The work has been registered in multiple repository instances, confirming its availability through open-access infrastructure. Without counts of downloads or event-based attention, the evidence of impact remains anchored to formal scholarly citation and institutional holding\u2014a pattern typical of specialized academic monographs in their early to mid-career phase.</p>",
